th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/boundary-of-a-set.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/boundary-of-a-set.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_boundary_of_a_set_69d73cc6_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In a metric space $(\\boldsymbol{X},d)$, what does it mean for $\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ to be a boundary point of $\\boldsymbol{E}\\subseteq \\boldsymbol{X}$?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ must lie inside $\\boldsymbol{E}$.","$\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ must lie outside $\\boldsymbol{E}$.","$\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ is the centre of a ball contained in $\\boldsymbol{E}$.","$\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ is neither interior nor exterior to $\\boldsymbol{E}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c83b16575e513931ae992c5c","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_boundary_of_a_set_69d73cc6_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the boundary of the interval $(0,1)$ in $\\mathbb{R}$ with the usual metric?","options":["$\\{0,1\\}$","$(0,1)$","$[0,1]$","$\\mathbb{R}\\setminus(0,1)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1f06e44ab1af6213139ff059","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_boundary_of_a_set_69d73cc6_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"In $\\mathbb{R}$, which point is a boundary point of $(0,1)$?","options":["$\\dfrac{1}{2}$","$2$","$1$","$-3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f59aab73c4429a5d127cb612","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_boundary_of_a_set_69d73cc6_006","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{G}\\subseteq\\boldsymbol{F}\\subseteq\\boldsymbol{E}$ in a metric space, and let $\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ be a boundary point of both $\\boldsymbol{G}$ and $\\boldsymbol{E}$. What follows?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ belongs to $\\boldsymbol{F}$.","$\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ is an interior point of $\\boldsymbol{F}$.","Nothing: $\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ may be exterior to $\\boldsymbol{F}$.","$\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ is a boundary point of $\\boldsymbol{F}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"681d78346edfca9b57b02c57","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_boundary_of_a_set_69d73cc6_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{E} = (0,1)\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}$. Which statement explains why $0$ is a boundary point of $\\boldsymbol{E}$?","options":["Every neighbourhood of $0$ meets both $(0,1)$ and its complement.","Every neighbourhood of $0$ meets $(0,1)$; the complement is not needed.","Some neighbourhood of $0$ lies entirely in $\\mathbb{R}\\setminus(0,1)$.","$0$ is a boundary point simply because it is not an element of $(0,1)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"66b9e1a226125dfb4198b9f4","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_boundary_of_a_set_69d73cc6_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"In $\\mathbb{R}$, what is $\\partial \\mathbb{Q}$?","options":["$\\mathbb{Q}$","$\\varnothing$","$\\mathbb{R}\\setminus\\mathbb{Q}$","$\\mathbb{R}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3c680af0030bd2e9cdbce9eb","time_limit":30},{"id":"expansion_boundary_of_a_set_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the boundary act like a dividing line?","options":["Every boundary point lies outside the set, next to its edge.","Every neighbourhood meets the set and its complement.","Some neighbourhood meets both the set and its complement.","Every neighbourhood meets the set, but may miss the complement."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3d615a661af94b1938434160","time_limit":30},{"id":"expansion_boundary_of_a_set_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $E=[0,1]$ in $\\mathbb{R}$, which statement about $0$ is correct?","options":["$0$ is a boundary point but cannot belong to $E$.","$0$ is exterior to $E$.","$0$ is a boundary point and belongs to $E$.","$0$ is interior to $E$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"eded3fe5a0e055ac3835a6d2","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_boundary_of_a_set_69d73cc6_009_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Suppose $x_0\\in X$ is not an interior point of $E\\subseteq X$. Why does this alone not make $x_0$ a boundary point of $E$?","options":["$x_0$ might be an exterior point: some ball around it misses $E$ entirely.","$x_0$ might belong to $E$, and points of $E$ are never boundary points.","$x_0$ might be an isolated point of $E$, and isolated points are not boundary points.","$x_0$ might be an adherent point of $E$, and adherent points are never boundary points."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"79418926f7cfe99ed49d47b6","time_limit":45},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_boundary_of_a_set_69d73cc6_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which identity holds for every metric space $(X,d)$ and every subset $E\\subseteq X$?","options":["$\\partial(X\\setminus E)=X\\setminus\\partial E$","$\\partial(X\\setminus E)=\\partial E\\setminus E$","$\\partial(X\\setminus E)=\\partial E$","$\\partial(X\\setminus E)\\cap\\partial E=\\varnothing$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"82b606562e3c8779a5003fb2","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_boundary_of_a_set_69d73cc6_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"In $\\mathbb{R}^2$ with the Euclidean distance, let $E=\\{\\boldsymbol{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\colon 0<\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|<1\\}$, the open unit disc with its centre $\\boldsymbol{0}$ removed. What is $\\partial E$?","options":["$\\{\\boldsymbol{x}\\colon\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|=1\\}$","$\\{\\boldsymbol{x}\\colon\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|=1\\}\\cup\\{\\boldsymbol{0}\\}$","$\\{\\boldsymbol{x}\\colon\\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\|\\le1\\}$","$\\{\\boldsymbol{0}\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"798942aaeb583d1bba5ba780","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_boundary_of_a_set_69d73cc6_topup20261028_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Equip $\\mathbb{R}$ with the discrete metric $d$, where $d(x,y)=1$ for $x\\neq y$ and $d(x,x)=0$. What is the boundary of $\\mathbb{Q}$ in $(\\mathbb{R},d)$?","options":["$\\varnothing$","$\\mathbb{R}$","$\\mathbb{R}\\setminus\\mathbb{Q}$","$\\mathbb{Q}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2d77d522572ace530c57da1e","time_limit":30}]}}